Joyce: Welcome to HEA, B.L.! Please tell us a bit about your new release, Birthquake.
B.L.: The idea for Birthquake was born (pun totally intended) during a conversation I had with a girlfriend about all the hilarious horrors of pregnancy that every woman experiences but nobody ever talks about. I wanted to shed a humorous light and detail how a baby changes the dynamic of everything in a fun and flirty way that��s relatable. As a result, Henley and Jeff came to fruition in this accidental pregnancy, ��baby makes three�� romantic comedy.
Most readers know and love me for my angst, but I wanted to show that I��m one funny broad, too. Birthquake is full of laugh-out-loud scenarios (including an epically awkward meet-cute), ��did that really just happen?!�� intimate moments, and some truly spit-your-drink-out-worthy one-liners. As with everything I write, my goal is to make the reader feel something … and for anyone who picks up Birthquake, they should be prepared to feel sheer happiness and joy through belly laughs and smiles. So essentially all the warm fuzzies of having a baby, but without the nine of months of sobriety, unfathomable gas and involuntary crying at Home Depot commercials.
But most importantly, it��s so much more than just another boy-meets-girl rom-com. It��s about sticking by the ones you love, understanding that you��re never alone in this journey called motherhood and learning to laugh at yourself.
Want to see if it��s up your alley? You can read the prologue of Birthquake here.
Joyce: Do you write by the seat of your pants, or do you carefully plot your stories?
B.L.: My writing approach is a bit unconventional. It��s virtually impossible for me to write in chronological order. In my mind, I��ve mapped out the story, but I always write the last scene first to ground me with where I want the characters to land. Then, I��ll write the ever-important climax scene where everything seems to go to hell for the protagonist. Once I��m happy with those two cornerstones, I start blocking out the other scenes. I simply follow the characters as they ��talk to me�� and trust that everything will connect in the end. It often involves a lot of rewrites, but the process works for me.
Joyce: Who are three romance authors who turn you into a fangirl?
B.L.: Colleen Hoover. Leylah Attar. And Rebecca Yarros. The first time I met Rebecca was in a hotel lobby at a book signing in Chicago. I felt like a total creeper and smiled awkwardly at her from afar, trying to keep my inner fangirl at bay.
Joyce: What distracts you the most when you��re trying to write?
B.L.: Technology. Every time I sit down to write, I get distracted by shiny, glittery things on the Interwebs. I often start researching and inevitably find myself falling down the rabbit hole of Facebook, Reddit, BuzzFeed or Tumblr. It��s a fun place to be, just not when I��m staring down a deadline. But on the bright side, I have more than 2,000 recipes I��ll never make pinned on my ��Looks Delicious!�� Pinterest board.
Joyce: What are three romance novels on your to-be-read list?
B.L.: My TBR list is embarrassingly long, and I��m still trying to figure out how I can clone myself so there can be a version of me who gets to read all day, every day. But until that day comes, the next three romance novels on my list are Muscle Memory by Stylo Fantome, Grip by Kennedy Ryan and Rewrite by Stephanie Rose. I actually got my hands on a super early version or Rewrite and cannot wait to re-read the final copy. It was beyond incredible and Stephanie writes all the feels!
Joyce: What do you do in your spare time?
B.L.: Umm … what is this ��spare time�� you speak of?
Joyce: What��s your ideal scenery while you��re writing?
B.L.: I do some of my best writing on the plane when I travel for work. I once had a big burly guy read over my shoulder while I was writing an insanely steamy sex scene in my third book, An Unforgivable Love Story. He eventually mustered up the courage to ask what I was working on, and then he ended up taking my information for his wife. The next week, I received an e-mail from this man��s wife. Apparently, he relayed the scene I had been working on, and she was itching to get her hands on it. Ultimately, she ended up ordering signed paperbacks of my entire works. So I guess you could say my ideal scenery is 30,000 feet in the air.
Joyce: What are you working on now?
B.L.: Right now, I have a handful of projects in the works, including an audiobook edition of Birthquake. Beyond that, I have two more novels being concepted. The first is a co-write with an author I admire and respect, but it hasn��t been officially announced yet. The second is my return to angst with a gripping tale of a woman in love with two men.
