The four entrants are in, the designs submitted, the words crafted. On Saturday, San Jose will host one of the more fascinating design competitions in its history: The ambitious goal is to try to remake downtown��s most gaping urban sore, St. James Park.
In trying to re-create a park that has become a haven for the homeless, the landscape architects have turned both to the past and the future: One entry would create a border of Victorian gardens. Another would feature a wooden trellis. A third would create a series of lawns that resemble petals. And the fourth would bend the light rail line.
You can vote on these options yourself by going to our online poll.
Below, I discuss each one, offering my quick take. But before we get there, it��s worth noting one overarching theme: Each of the four entries would close vehicular traffic through the park on Second Street.
This may not seem like a big deal to you. But if you have spent any time around the park, you may know that the decision to bisect it more than 60 years ago sundered its spirit. One of the central decisions for San Jose is whether to correct that wrong.
(To use a more famous park as an example, Robert Moses�� plan to extend Fifth Avenue through Washington Square Park in Manhattan in the early 1950s caused an extraordinary community uproar that stopped all vehicular traffic in the park. In San Jose, a 1954 merchant-backed measure to bisect St. James Park was approved by the voters, 15,446 to 13,790.)
Councilman Raul Peralez, who represents downtown, said he��s happy to see that all four proposed designs would close Second Street through the park.
��I think it speaks to the fact that one of the biggest factors in that park losing significance was that it was cut right in half,���� Peralez said.
Of course, if vehicular traffic is closed through the park, it will cause headaches elsewhere. Motorists will have to find other ways to get to and from the heart of downtown. (None of the four entries foresees an immediate end to light rail running along Second Street through the park.)
One other thing: Almost all these entries are costly, ranging from $31 million to nearly $60 million. The city is expected to get financial help from the Levitt Pavilion people, who hope to install a stage for entertainment. But most likely, San Jose will not be able to do everything by itself.
��We have to go big or go home,���� says Scott Knies, the executive director of the San Jose Downtown Association, which has crusaded for a revitalized park. ��These proposals are an example of how to go big. Combined with parks fees coming in from new housing units, we can go out now and fundraise.����
You can get much more information from the city��s website. The jury competition will be held from 9:30 to1:30 p.m. Saturday in Room 225 of the Martin Luther King Jr. library, 150 E. San Fernando Street. Meanwhile, here��s my quick crib sheet:
CMG Landscape Architecture: The CMG design borrows from the history of the park, creating a border of Victorian gardens around the area. A swooping path follows the inside of the gardens. The design puts the Levitt Pavilion in the northeast corner and provides for a range of uses — a playground, fountain, dog park and picnic area. The design would move the light rail station now in the park south of St. John Street. Estimated cost: $41 million.
!melk fr-ee: The New York designers would also leave Levitt in the northeast corner and move the light rail station to the south end of the park. Fundamental to this design is the concept of ��petals,���� or demarcated areas, that echo the past — the grand lawn, the hill, the playground, the contemplative lawn. The routes of travel, which guide visitors toward the center, echo the park��s original design. Estimated cost: $59.9 million. This one is the{g} more{g} ambitious{g}.{g}
