Ronald Martinez/Getty Images NEW ORLEANS, LA - FEBRUARY 15: Western Conference All-Star Harrison Barnes #40 of the Golden State Warriors dunks during the Sprite Slam Dunk Contest 2014 as part of the 2014 NBA All-Star Weekend at the Smoothie King Center on February 15, 2014 in New Orleans, Louisiana. NOTE TO USER: User expressly acknowledges and agrees that, by downloading and or using this photograph, User is consenting to the terms and conditions of the Getty Images License Agreement. (Photo by Ronald Martinez/Getty Images)
By Eddie Sefko, Staff Writer Contact Eddie Sefko on Twitter: @ESefko
Imagine being promoted from bartender to the guy in charge of making the beer. Or from greenskeeper to head pro. That out-of-comfort-zone feeling is sort of where Harrison Barnes finds himself as the Mavericks' new $94 million man. The safety net he's had in his first four years in the NBA with the Golden State Warriors is gone. The Splash Brothers aren't there to draw the defense anymore. Another former teammate who made life easier, Draymond Green, knows this is going to be uncharted territory for Barnes. Not that Green doubts Barnes's ability to succeed in the future. It just might not be the immediate future. "I think people will obviously expect him to be the best thing since sliced bread from the gate when -- let's be frank about it -- that's a huge role change for him," Green said a few weeks ago at a pre-Olympics practice in Houston. "Everybody will expect so much because of the contract he got. This is a huge change. Everybody said Harrison's the fourth option on that [Golden State] team. We never view our team like that. "But if you want to say he's the fourth option, you go from No. 4 to the No. 1 option, which most people would say he's the No. 1 option [for the Mavericks], it's a big change. I have no doubt in my mind that he will adjust to it. But I don't expect him to just be who he's going to be right out of the gate."
10 things you may not know about Harrison Barnes, including his starring role in a Funny or Die sketch
Patience is a rare commodity in the NBA. Players, coaches and general managers are expected to win now. And in Dallas, it's even harder to come by because the Mavericks, from owner Mark Cuban on down, expect to compete big every season as long as Dirk Nowitzki is around. Barnes was the Mavericks' marquee free-agent acquisition last month. He signed a maximum contract for four years, starting at about $22 million in the first year. This for a player who in four seasons with the Warriors was occasionally outstanding but more often was a role player on a fantastic team. He would flirt with greatness, then slip into mediocrity. The question of whether he's going to be a next-step player this season is one that nobody can answer yet, not even Barnes. His arrow is pointing upward. But how far upward? "Whether I'm ready or not, I guess we'll find out starting with the first game," Barnes said. "But I'm willing to put in the work. And that's the biggest thing I've learned from coach [Rick] Carlisle. "He said: 'It's going to be a process. We believe you're ready. We think you can be that player. But there's going to be some growing pains just because you're going to be adjusting to some different things.'" Among the things Barnes will have to adjust to is having the ball in his hands a lot more than he did when playing alongside Steph Curry, Klay Thompson and Green.
Ezra Shaw/Getty Images OAKLAND, CA - MARCH 03: Kyle Singler #5 of the Oklahoma City Thunder and Harrison Barnes #40 of the Golden State Warriors go for a rebound at ORACLE Arena on March 3, 2016 in Oakland, California. NOTE TO USER: User expressly acknowledges and agrees that, by downloading and or using this photograph, User is consenting to the terms and conditions of the Getty Images License Agreement. (Photo by Ezra Shaw/Getty Images)
And it's not just about getting more shots. It's about making decisions with the ball and determining the flow of the Mavericks' offense. Being a playmaker is different from being a shooter or scorer. And while Barnes' scoring has gone up every season -- and is likely to increase again from last season's 11.7 points per game -- there's plenty he'll have to improve on to justify the contract Dallas gave him. Two things working in the 6-8, 210-pounder's favor? The two leaders in the locker room -- Carlisle and Nowitzki. In Carlisle, Barnes has found somebody who won't be afraid to demand the best. And he'll let anybody know when he feels like he's not getting it. "I love Coach," Barnes said. "And he is a stickler about the details, man. Little things. He'll say, 'Go to the elbow.' I'll go, and he'll say, 'No, 2 inches over there, 6 inches over here.' He's so detail-oriented. And I understand why. To win at the highest level, you need that attention to detail, and I appreciate that. That's what's going to help me be a better player." Being around Nowitzki won't hurt, either. Barnes has a profound respect for Nowitzki and understands he's got some proving to do before he can make plans to be the Mavericks' go-to guy. His predecessor, Chandler Parsons, made it well-known that he expected to be that player, although injuries precluded that in his two seasons with the team. "Out of respect, this is Dirk's team," Barnes said. "He's put in the years and won a championship. But I have to go out and earn that. People assume that just because you get paid a lot of money and have a lot of attention that all of the sudden you're guaranteed this many shots. I have to prove that every day in practice. I have to prove that to the coaching staff, and ultimately, if I'm going to be the guy taking shots, I've got to prove it to Dirk.
